WEBVTT

00:04.650 --> 00:06.925
This is Allen Company first-aid training on eye

00:06.925 --> 00:10.412
injuries and chemical exposures. At a quarry,

00:10.412 --> 00:13.350
eyes get hit by dust, flying rock chips,

00:13.350 --> 00:16.200
grinding sparks, and chemical splashes.

00:16.200 --> 00:18.438
Safety glasses prevent most of them.

00:18.438 --> 00:20.825
But when one happens, what you do in the first

00:20.825 --> 00:21.938
minute matters.

00:23.065 --> 00:25.002
This is first-aid awareness training.

00:25.002 --> 00:28.489
It follows first-aid methods acceptable to MSHA,

00:28.489 --> 00:31.040
based on the American Heart Association first-aid

00:31.040 --> 00:33.902
guidelines that Allen uses for hands-on training.

00:33.902 --> 00:36.864
It supports, but does not replace, your hands-on

00:36.864 --> 00:39.227
first-aid practice with the safety manager,

00:39.227 --> 00:41.202
the same day as this course.

00:44.556 --> 00:47.368
For dust or a small particle, don't rub.

00:47.368 --> 00:51.231
Let tears work, or flush with clean water or eyewash,

00:51.231 --> 00:54.031
from the inner corner outward. If it's still there,

00:54.031 --> 00:56.556
or vision is blurry, get medical care.

00:57.688 --> 00:59.713
If something is stuck in the eye, don't try to

00:59.713 --> 01:02.675
remove it. Protect it with a cup or a padded ring

01:02.675 --> 01:05.425
without pressing on the eye, cover both eyes to

01:05.425 --> 01:08.175
limit movement, and get them to medical care.

01:09.335 --> 01:12.348
For a blow to the eye, a cold pack without pressure

01:12.348 --> 01:15.873
for 15 minutes. Any vision change, pain,

01:15.873 --> 01:17.898
or blood in the eye means a doctor.

01:21.262 --> 01:23.887
For a chemical splash to the eye, every second

01:23.887 --> 01:26.762
counts. Get to the eyewash right away,

01:26.762 --> 01:29.674
hold the eyelids open, and rinse with lots of water

01:29.674 --> 01:32.450
for at least 15 minutes, or until medical help

01:32.450 --> 01:36.187
arrives, rolling the eyes so water reaches everywhere.

01:36.187 --> 01:38.937
Take out contact lenses if they come out easily.

01:38.937 --> 01:41.774
Our eyewash stations are bottles. At Boonesboro

01:41.774 --> 01:43.962
they're at the scale house, breakroom,

01:43.962 --> 01:47.462
shop and the primary crusher MCC. At Dix River,

01:47.462 --> 01:50.587
the scale house. At Clover Bottom, the scale house

01:50.587 --> 01:52.862
and breakroom. If the bottle runs out,

01:52.862 --> 01:55.787
keep rinsing with clean tap water. Then call poison

01:55.787 --> 01:59.299
control or get medical care, and take the SDS.

02:00.484 --> 02:04.122
For skin contact, flush with water and remove contaminated

02:04.122 --> 02:06.760
clothing. If someone breathed in fumes,

02:06.760 --> 02:09.334
get them to fresh air, but don't go into a bad

02:09.334 --> 02:12.447
atmosphere yourself. If someone swallowed a chemical,

02:12.447 --> 02:17.959
call Poison Control at 1-800-222-1222 and follow

02:17.959 --> 02:21.260
the SDS; don't make them vomit unless told to.

02:24.576 --> 02:27.464
Recap: wear your glasses. Don't rub.

02:27.464 --> 02:30.638
Don't pull out embedded objects; cover both eyes.

02:30.638 --> 02:34.138
Chemical in the eye: eyewash now, 15 minutes.

02:34.138 --> 02:36.726
Use the SDS and get medical care.

02:37.842 --> 02:41.004
Remember: your site's plans and procedures always

02:41.004 --> 02:42.505
override this video.
